1
00:00:00,520 --> 00:00:03,278
Right now, there are hundreds of millions

2
00:00:03,148 --> 00:00:05,683
of cats living with humans all over

3
00:00:05,513 --> 00:00:06,266
the planet.

4
00:00:06,579 --> 00:00:08,650
But cats weren't always on our sofas.

5
00:00:09,695 --> 00:00:13,071
Ten thousand years ago, your cat's ancestors

6
00:00:12,937 --> 00:00:15,241
were wild hunters in the desert.

7
00:00:15,606 --> 00:00:17,082
So how did they end up ruling

8
00:00:16,946 --> 00:00:17,942
our homes?

9
00:00:18,983 --> 00:00:21,495
Today, we travel through the secret history

10
00:00:21,355 --> 00:00:22,205
of cats:

11
00:00:22,539 --> 00:00:25,815
from wild deserts to ancient Egypt, Viking

12
00:00:25,725 --> 00:00:30,122
ships, Japanese temples, and even outer space.

13
00:00:31,141 --> 00:00:31,970
Chapter one:

14
00:00:32,354 --> 00:00:33,916
the wild ancestor.

15
00:00:34,251 --> 00:00:36,703
Every pet cat on Earth descends from

16
00:00:36,606 --> 00:00:37,924
one wild cat:

17
00:00:38,263 --> 00:00:41,091
the African wildcat, from the Near East

18
00:00:41,140 --> 00:00:42,407
and North Africa.

19
00:00:43,464 --> 00:00:47,373
In 2007, scientists compared the DNA of

20
00:00:47,258 --> 00:00:49,636
nearly a thousand cats from around the

21
00:00:49,490 --> 00:00:50,042
world.

22
00:00:50,357 --> 00:00:52,390
They all led back to this same

23
00:00:52,419 --> 00:00:53,709
desert wildcat.

24
00:00:54,682 --> 00:00:57,514
It's a small, sandy-colored cat with faint

25
00:00:57,402 --> 00:00:58,223
stripes.

26
00:00:58,561 --> 00:01:00,333
It looks a lot like a skinny

27
00:01:00,213 --> 00:01:03,106
house tabby, and that's no coincidence.

28
00:01:04,111 --> 00:01:09,519
African wildcats are solitary, shy, and amazing

29
00:01:09,418 --> 00:01:12,105
hunters of mice and small animals.

30
00:01:12,421 --> 00:01:14,784
And that hunting skill is about to

31
00:01:14,654 --> 00:01:16,051
change everything.

32
00:01:17,005 --> 00:01:20,347
Because around ten thousand years ago, humans

33
00:01:20,207 --> 00:01:21,801
started doing something new:

34
00:01:22,416 --> 00:01:23,079
farming.

35
00:01:24,059 --> 00:01:25,336
And here's the twist.

36
00:01:25,725 --> 00:01:28,553
Humans didn't capture wild cats and tame

37
00:01:28,422 --> 00:01:28,792
them.

38
00:01:29,107 --> 00:01:31,065
In a way, cats chose us.

39
00:01:32,093 --> 00:01:32,960
Chapter two:

40
00:01:33,190 --> 00:01:35,233
the farmers and the mice.

41
00:01:35,589 --> 00:01:37,814
When people in the Fertile Crescent began

42
00:01:37,706 --> 00:01:40,240
growing grain, they stored it in big

43
00:01:40,150 --> 00:01:41,777
piles and granaries.

44
00:01:42,767 --> 00:01:45,143
Stored grain attracted mice.

45
00:01:45,490 --> 00:01:46,457
Lots of mice.

46
00:01:46,793 --> 00:01:49,884
And where there are mice, hungry wildcats

47
00:01:49,918 --> 00:01:50,964
soon follow.

48
00:01:51,973 --> 00:01:53,752
The cats that weren't too afraid of

49
00:01:53,599 --> 00:01:55,561
people got an easy buffet.

50
00:01:55,906 --> 00:01:59,408
They stayed close to villages, hunted, and

51
00:01:59,274 --> 00:02:00,717
had more kittens.

52
00:02:01,754 --> 00:02:04,504
Farmers loved it, because the cats protected

53
00:02:04,358 --> 00:02:06,064
their food for free.

54
00:02:06,419 --> 00:02:07,893
Nobody needed to train them.

55
00:02:08,216 --> 00:02:09,814
Both sides simply won.

56
00:02:10,827 --> 00:02:14,634
Over many generations, the bolder, calmer cats

57
00:02:14,510 --> 00:02:17,032
became a new kind of cat, one

58
00:02:16,909 --> 00:02:18,682
that could live side by side with

59
00:02:18,552 --> 00:02:19,297
humans.

60
00:02:20,311 --> 00:02:22,461
The oldest evidence we have comes from

61
00:02:22,361 --> 00:02:23,342
Cyprus:

62
00:02:23,732 --> 00:02:26,469
a grave about nine thousand five hundred

63
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:28,780
years old, where a person was buried

64
00:02:28,636 --> 00:02:29,469
with a cat.

65
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:33,462
And since Cyprus has no native wildcats,

66
00:02:33,657 --> 00:02:36,029
someone must have carried that cat across

67
00:02:35,878 --> 00:02:36,625
the sea.

68
00:02:37,027 --> 00:02:39,118
Cats were already traveling with us.

69
00:02:40,180 --> 00:02:41,262
Chapter three:

70
00:02:41,740 --> 00:02:42,848
ancient Egypt.

71
00:02:43,197 --> 00:02:45,340
If you think people love cats today,

72
00:02:45,815 --> 00:02:48,597
wait until you meet the ancient Egyptians.

73
00:02:49,609 --> 00:02:53,223
In Egypt, cats protected homes and grain

74
00:02:53,112 --> 00:02:54,634
from mice and snakes.

75
00:02:54,982 --> 00:02:57,755
Soon they appeared in paintings, sitting under

76
00:02:57,637 --> 00:03:01,434
chairs, wearing collars, and even joining hunting

77
00:03:01,318 --> 00:03:02,007
trips.

78
00:03:03,014 --> 00:03:05,501
Cats became linked to the goddess Bastet,

79
00:03:05,679 --> 00:03:09,173
a protector of homes, mothers and children,

80
00:03:09,511 --> 00:03:11,035
often shown with the head of a

81
00:03:10,949 --> 00:03:11,490
cat.

82
00:03:12,490 --> 00:03:15,598
The city of Bubastis held huge festivals

83
00:03:15,461 --> 00:03:18,513
for Bastet, and temples there kept cats

84
00:03:18,549 --> 00:03:19,890
as sacred animals.

85
00:03:20,872 --> 00:03:24,156
According to the Greek historian Herodotus, when

86
00:03:23,993 --> 00:03:27,475
a family cat died, Egyptians shaved off

87
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:29,712
their eyebrows as a sign of mourning.

88
00:03:30,686 --> 00:03:33,360
Egyptians even gave their cats names.

89
00:03:33,672 --> 00:03:35,307
One of the oldest cat names we

90
00:03:35,204 --> 00:03:38,393
know, found in an ancient tomb, is

91
00:03:38,278 --> 00:03:41,339
Nedjem, which means sweet or pleasant.

92
00:03:42,313 --> 00:03:44,835
Many cats were mummified and buried with

93
00:03:44,717 --> 00:03:45,300
care.

94
00:03:45,635 --> 00:03:48,174
Archaeologists have found cat mummies by the

95
00:03:48,041 --> 00:03:49,662
hundreds of thousands.

96
00:03:50,671 --> 00:03:52,949
Ancient writers even claimed that harming a

97
00:03:52,836 --> 00:03:55,239
cat in Egypt could get you into

98
00:03:55,150 --> 00:03:56,735
very serious trouble.

99
00:03:57,787 --> 00:04:00,897
For many cats, ancient Egypt really was

100
00:04:00,742 --> 00:04:01,874
the golden age.

101
00:04:02,235 --> 00:04:04,106
It's also one of the main places

102
00:04:04,013 --> 00:04:06,667
cats spread out from, to the rest

103
00:04:06,517 --> 00:04:07,231
of the world.

104
00:04:08,241 --> 00:04:09,221
Chapter four:

105
00:04:09,583 --> 00:04:10,546
cats at sea.

106
00:04:10,907 --> 00:04:14,417
Ships carried grain, and grain attracted rats.

107
00:04:14,812 --> 00:04:17,125
So sailors brought along the best pest

108
00:04:17,007 --> 00:04:17,991
control they knew:

109
00:04:18,601 --> 00:04:19,333
cats.

110
00:04:20,386 --> 00:04:23,045
Cats traveled on trading ships across the

111
00:04:22,894 --> 00:04:26,611
Mediterranean, and from there, along trade routes

112
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:29,259
to Europe, Asia and beyond.

113
00:04:30,261 --> 00:04:33,670
A big 2017 genetic study found ancient

114
00:04:33,578 --> 00:04:37,325
cat remains all along those routes, even

115
00:04:37,186 --> 00:04:39,374
at a Viking trading port in northern

116
00:04:39,234 --> 00:04:39,897
Germany.

117
00:04:40,913 --> 00:04:42,826
Yes, Vikings had cats.

118
00:04:43,171 --> 00:04:45,578
They probably kept them on board and

119
00:04:45,474 --> 00:04:48,135
in their settlements to protect food and

120
00:04:48,040 --> 00:04:49,569
furs from rodents.

121
00:04:50,558 --> 00:04:53,784
For centuries after that, almost every ship

122
00:04:53,679 --> 00:04:54,922
had its ship's cat.

123
00:04:55,286 --> 00:04:57,517
Many sailors believed a cat on board

124
00:04:57,397 --> 00:04:58,528
brought good luck.

125
00:04:59,541 --> 00:05:01,530
Ship's cats were so useful that the

126
00:05:01,372 --> 00:05:04,243
British Royal Navy kept cats on board

127
00:05:04,114 --> 00:05:05,195
for centuries.

128
00:05:05,519 --> 00:05:07,585
They were only banned from its ships

129
00:05:07,658 --> 00:05:09,183
in 1975.

130
00:05:10,251 --> 00:05:12,277
And that's how a desert cat from

131
00:05:12,117 --> 00:05:15,128
the Near East ended up on every

132
00:05:14,994 --> 00:05:16,917
continent except Antarctica.

133
00:05:17,860 --> 00:05:18,913
Chapter five:

134
00:05:19,229 --> 00:05:20,463
the dark times.

135
00:05:20,856 --> 00:05:23,704
Not every era was kind to cats.

136
00:05:24,058 --> 00:05:26,330
In parts of medieval Europe, cats got

137
00:05:26,207 --> 00:05:27,539
a bad reputation.

138
00:05:28,535 --> 00:05:31,651
Their independence, their glowing eyes and their

139
00:05:31,526 --> 00:05:34,280
night habits made some people link them,

140
00:05:34,652 --> 00:05:38,198
especially black cats, to witchcraft and bad

141
00:05:38,110 --> 00:05:38,619
luck.

142
00:05:39,646 --> 00:05:42,861
In some places, cats were persecuted during

143
00:05:42,735 --> 00:05:45,107
festivals and witch panics.

144
00:05:45,423 --> 00:05:46,540
It was a hard time to be

145
00:05:46,396 --> 00:05:47,020
a cat.

146
00:05:48,026 --> 00:05:49,835
You may have heard that killing cats

147
00:05:49,718 --> 00:05:51,937
made the plague worse, because there were

148
00:05:51,812 --> 00:05:52,932
more rats.

149
00:05:53,302 --> 00:05:56,150
Historians still debate how true that is,

150
00:05:56,511 --> 00:05:57,755
so take it with a grain of

151
00:05:57,635 --> 00:05:58,208
salt.

152
00:05:59,228 --> 00:06:01,717
But superstition never fully won.

153
00:06:02,152 --> 00:06:06,333
Farmers, millers and sailors kept cats, because

154
00:06:06,194 --> 00:06:08,899
nothing beat them at catching rodents.

155
00:06:09,909 --> 00:06:10,998
Chapter six:

156
00:06:11,386 --> 00:06:12,457
cats in Japan.

157
00:06:12,805 --> 00:06:15,911
Cats probably reached Japan more than a

158
00:06:15,767 --> 00:06:19,672
thousand years ago, likely protecting precious books

159
00:06:19,741 --> 00:06:21,387
and scrolls from mice.

160
00:06:22,408 --> 00:06:26,156
Later, silk farmers valued cats because mice

161
00:06:26,167 --> 00:06:27,687
threatened the silkworms.

162
00:06:28,023 --> 00:06:30,986
Some even kept cat charms and statues

163
00:06:30,871 --> 00:06:32,250
to keep the mice away.

164
00:06:33,230 --> 00:06:36,210
And then there's the famous maneki-neko, the

165
00:06:36,053 --> 00:06:38,952
beckoning cat, with one paw raised.

166
00:06:39,289 --> 00:06:41,029
You've probably seen it in shops and

167
00:06:40,878 --> 00:06:41,828
restaurants.

168
00:06:42,782 --> 00:06:45,392
It's believed to bring luck and customers.

169
00:06:45,692 --> 00:06:47,352
A raised left paw is said to

170
00:06:47,231 --> 00:06:49,860
invite people in, and a raised right

171
00:06:49,757 --> 00:06:51,802
paw is said to invite money.

172
00:06:52,825 --> 00:06:56,277
Today, Japan even has cat islands, like

173
00:06:56,167 --> 00:06:59,839
Tashirojima, where cats outnumber people and are

174
00:06:59,711 --> 00:07:01,772
treated as local treasures.

175
00:07:02,785 --> 00:07:06,391
Japanese folklore also has darker cat legends,

176
00:07:06,673 --> 00:07:10,398
like the bakeneko and the nekomata, magical

177
00:07:10,297 --> 00:07:12,520
cats said to have strange powers.

178
00:07:13,546 --> 00:07:14,668
Chapter seven:

179
00:07:14,986 --> 00:07:17,063
famous cats in history.

180
00:07:17,412 --> 00:07:20,322
Some cats became true celebrities.

181
00:07:21,384 --> 00:07:25,246
In 1963, a French cat named Felicette

182
00:07:25,245 --> 00:07:27,643
flew to space on a rocket.

183
00:07:27,973 --> 00:07:30,320
She came back safely, and she's still

184
00:07:30,165 --> 00:07:31,825
the only cat to have flown to

185
00:07:31,714 --> 00:07:32,539
space.

186
00:07:33,550 --> 00:07:36,761
In the small town of Talkeetna, Alaska,

187
00:07:37,162 --> 00:07:39,976
a cat named Stubbs was the honorary

188
00:07:39,868 --> 00:07:41,743
mayor for about twenty years.

189
00:07:42,092 --> 00:07:43,631
Tourists came just to meet him.

190
00:07:44,608 --> 00:07:47,214
In London, a cat named Larry has

191
00:07:47,089 --> 00:07:49,697
held the official title of Chief Mouser

192
00:07:49,577 --> 00:07:52,135
to the Cabinet Office, living at 10

193
00:07:52,057 --> 00:07:54,912
Downing Street since 2011.

194
00:07:55,946 --> 00:07:58,803
He has outlasted several prime ministers.

195
00:07:59,118 --> 00:08:01,185
Prime ministers come and go, but the

196
00:08:01,072 --> 00:08:02,284
cat stays.

197
00:08:03,304 --> 00:08:05,980
And many famous writers, from Mark Twain

198
00:08:05,869 --> 00:08:09,480
to Ernest Hemingway, were well-known cat lovers.

199
00:08:09,807 --> 00:08:12,547
Hemingway's home in Florida is still full

200
00:08:12,421 --> 00:08:13,531
of cats today.

201
00:08:14,496 --> 00:08:17,285
Even Isaac Newton is often credited with

202
00:08:17,136 --> 00:08:18,732
inventing the cat flap.

203
00:08:19,071 --> 00:08:21,193
It's a fun story, but there's no

204
00:08:21,123 --> 00:08:22,791
real proof that he did.

205
00:08:23,811 --> 00:08:24,800
Chapter eight:

206
00:08:24,974 --> 00:08:26,076
the modern cat.

207
00:08:26,453 --> 00:08:29,009
For most of history, cats weren't bred

208
00:08:28,878 --> 00:08:29,744
for looks.

209
00:08:30,085 --> 00:08:32,774
They were shaped by nature, for hunting.

210
00:08:33,784 --> 00:08:36,938
That changed in 1871, at the first

211
00:08:36,837 --> 00:08:39,215
big cat show, held at the Crystal

212
00:08:39,084 --> 00:08:40,247
Palace in London.

213
00:08:40,594 --> 00:08:43,778
Suddenly, people cared about fur, color and

214
00:08:43,659 --> 00:08:44,290
shape.

215
00:08:45,298 --> 00:08:47,009
Most of the cat breeds we know

216
00:08:46,904 --> 00:08:49,498
today were created in the last one

217
00:08:49,339 --> 00:08:50,901
hundred and fifty years.

218
00:08:51,935 --> 00:08:53,731
Some breeds are much older, though.

219
00:08:54,047 --> 00:08:56,100
The Siamese, like the cat on this

220
00:08:55,966 --> 00:08:59,315
channel, comes from Thailand, the old kingdom

221
00:08:59,169 --> 00:09:02,074
of Siam, and appears in old Thai

222
00:09:01,961 --> 00:09:03,104
manuscripts.

223
00:09:04,106 --> 00:09:06,579
Siamese cats arrived in Britain in the

224
00:09:06,437 --> 00:09:09,743
late eighteen hundreds, and people were amazed

225
00:09:09,631 --> 00:09:12,411
by their blue eyes and dark points.

226
00:09:13,377 --> 00:09:15,235
And the Maine Coon, one of the

227
00:09:15,213 --> 00:09:18,485
biggest domestic cat breeds, is the official

228
00:09:18,361 --> 00:09:20,617
state cat of Maine in the United

229
00:09:20,511 --> 00:09:21,279
States.

230
00:09:22,291 --> 00:09:24,880
Today, there are hundreds of millions of

231
00:09:24,756 --> 00:09:26,941
cats in the world, and in many

232
00:09:26,837 --> 00:09:29,744
countries, cats are the most popular pet.

233
00:09:30,772 --> 00:09:31,648
Chapter nine:

234
00:09:31,997 --> 00:09:33,170
still a little wild.

235
00:09:33,536 --> 00:09:35,116
Here's the fascinating part.

236
00:09:35,467 --> 00:09:38,838
Genetically, house cats are still very close

237
00:09:38,733 --> 00:09:40,485
to their wild ancestors.

238
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:43,625
Dogs changed a lot as they lived

239
00:09:43,485 --> 00:09:44,461
with humans.

240
00:09:44,823 --> 00:09:46,369
Cats changed much less.

241
00:09:46,717 --> 00:09:49,010
They mainly became more tolerant of us,

242
00:09:49,383 --> 00:09:50,895
and a bit more sociable.

243
00:09:51,927 --> 00:09:55,204
Domestic cats even have slightly smaller brains

244
00:09:55,065 --> 00:09:57,807
than wildcats, which is common in tame

245
00:09:57,696 --> 00:09:58,480
animals.

246
00:09:58,824 --> 00:10:00,713
But on the outside, they look almost

247
00:10:00,554 --> 00:10:01,299
the same.

248
00:10:02,315 --> 00:10:03,993
That's why your cat can be a

249
00:10:03,868 --> 00:10:06,869
cuddly couch potato one minute, and a

250
00:10:06,738 --> 00:10:08,111
fierce hunter the next.

251
00:10:09,094 --> 00:10:13,658
Many scientists describe cats as only semi-domesticated.

252
00:10:13,981 --> 00:10:16,551
Put a house cat back outside, and

253
00:10:16,399 --> 00:10:18,267
it can often survive on its own.

254
00:10:19,261 --> 00:10:22,142
In other words, cats never fully gave

255
00:10:22,027 --> 00:10:23,291
up their independence.

256
00:10:23,602 --> 00:10:25,752
They live with us because they want

257
00:10:25,678 --> 00:10:26,013
to.

258
00:10:27,042 --> 00:10:28,543
So the next time your cat sleeps

259
00:10:28,409 --> 00:10:30,374
on your keyboard, remember:

260
00:10:30,947 --> 00:10:33,155
you're looking at ten thousand years of

261
00:10:33,044 --> 00:10:36,465
history, from desert hunter to sacred animal

262
00:10:36,527 --> 00:10:38,567
to sailor to space traveler.

263
00:10:39,535 --> 00:10:41,284
We didn't really choose cats.

264
00:10:41,681 --> 00:10:44,167
Cats chose us, and they've been running

265
00:10:43,998 --> 00:10:45,367
the show ever since.

266
00:10:46,363 --> 00:10:48,794
Which part of cat history surprised you

267
00:10:48,631 --> 00:10:49,365
the most?

268
00:10:49,717 --> 00:10:52,212
Tell me in the comments, and subscribe

269
00:10:52,266 --> 00:10:53,799
for more cat stories.
